00:04:59.996 --> 00:05:05.756
So one of the other problems that people have is they don't
know exactly what's wrong with your agent, right?

00:05:05.796 --> 00:05:08.916
They will say, my agent is slow.
My agent doesn't do what it's supposed to do.

00:05:08.916 --> 00:05:13.836
But how do we fix this? Right? And what's very
important is you can't fix what you can't see.

00:05:14.076 --> 00:05:20.676
And this is where observability comes in. Observability
will help us trace step by step what your agent is doing,

00:05:20.676 --> 00:05:25.716
what tools are being used, as well
as what is the latencies between each of these things.

00:05:26.596 --> 00:05:33.716
And on our side, we have something called AgentCore Observability
that outputs all of these traces into an open telemetry format.

00:05:33.916 --> 00:05:40.516
This lets us step through each of these traces in a way
that we are able to break it down step by step, call by call.

00:05:41.996 --> 00:05:49.956
And now that we have observability, right, we now want to think
about how do I prove that my agent is working well or not well,

00:05:49.956 --> 00:05:52.716
and especially between different versions of the agent.

00:05:52.716 --> 00:05:59.636
That's where evaluation comes in. There are two types
of evaluation that we think of on demand evaluation.

00:05:59.836 --> 00:06:02.436
This happens during your development phase.

00:06:02.436 --> 00:06:04.836
So how do I prove the agent fundamentally works?

00:06:04.836 --> 00:06:08.796
I built a evaluation data set. I test against it and it works.

00:06:08.796 --> 00:06:11.836
Then I launch it right. And then there's online evaluation.

00:06:11.836 --> 00:06:15.796
That's you running it on a small
percentage of all of your operational agents.

00:06:15.796 --> 00:06:22.436
So this way we can monitor agents in real time to see if
there's any unexpected behavior or if there's some cost overruns.

00:06:24.396 --> 00:06:27.156
But there are many, many evaluation metrics that you can do.

00:06:27.156 --> 00:06:30.396
These are just some of the things that AgentCore evaluation has.

00:06:30.396 --> 00:06:35.156
There are 13 different metrics,
but some of the key ones like the session level metrics.

00:06:35.156 --> 00:06:38.436
So did my agent even accomplish its task or not?

00:06:38.476 --> 00:06:41.756
Right. The trace level metrics. So these are individual parts of it.

00:06:41.756 --> 00:06:44.836
So we can see whether it actually managed to help the user or not.

00:06:44.836 --> 00:06:50.996
Or two level metrics, which means that am I using
the most or using the tools in the most efficient way?

00:06:50.996 --> 00:06:55.436
I'm not going between three different
tools and then swapping them every single time.

00:06:55.436 --> 00:07:00.436
So now I'll hand it over to Voss
to talk pretty much about the most important things.

00:07:02.356 --> 00:07:03.956
Thank you very much, Isaac.

00:07:05.356 --> 00:07:09.676
And now we'll talk about what keeps leaders up at night cost.

00:07:10.076 --> 00:07:17.356
So we'll be talking about five different cost optimization
strategies that you can use for your Generative AI application.

00:07:17.956 --> 00:07:20.956
And the first one is actually deceptively simple.

00:07:21.236 --> 00:07:23.996
Start with a use case, not the model.

00:07:24.556 --> 00:07:29.436
I see a lot of customers being fixated
with the latest and greatest frontier model.

00:07:29.676 --> 00:07:35.516
And this actually makes sense when we're talking about POC,
because there you want to show the art of the possible.

00:07:35.516 --> 00:07:42.596
But then when you're when you are in production,
you have to use you have to demonstrate ROI, right?

00:07:42.596 --> 00:07:48.196
And which means that you have to use the cheapest
model that meets the bar for your use case.

00:07:48.476 --> 00:07:53.076
Which means you have to be able to evaluate multiple candidates,

00:07:53.116 --> 00:07:57.116
candidates of models and select the one that meets these requirements.

00:07:57.236 --> 00:08:00.796
So don't be forced to always jump into the latest and greatest.

00:08:00.836 --> 00:08:02.596
Choose the model wisely.

00:08:04.076 --> 00:08:08.196
The second one is that you have to choose the correct inference mode,

00:08:08.196 --> 00:08:16.436
and many of you may be familiar with the token based
pricing of Amazon Bedrock, which is near real time.

00:08:16.476 --> 00:08:21.556
This is actually the standard tier and is good for everyday AI tasks.

00:08:21.556 --> 00:08:28.476
But how about, for example, some use cases where you can afford
to wait a little bit more for the for your results, right?

00:08:28.756 --> 00:08:34.156
For this kind of cases, we have two
additional inference modes flex and batch.

00:08:34.396 --> 00:08:40.836
And the trade off here is that actually you can wait a little bit more
to get your results, but you pay half the price.

00:08:40.836 --> 00:08:48.316
Now, on the opposite side of the spectrum, there could be some use
cases where having low latency is of paramount importance.

00:08:48.716 --> 00:08:58.116
For these kind of cases, we have two additional inference
mode tears the reserved and the priority and the trade off.

00:08:58.156 --> 00:08:59.956
There is actually the opposite, right?

00:08:59.956 --> 00:09:04.156
So you're paying a little bit more
in order to get your results faster.

00:09:04.156 --> 00:09:08.476
So again, don't blindly always jump to the standard tier.

00:09:08.516 --> 00:09:12.596
Think about your use case and select the right inference mode.

00:09:13.716 --> 00:09:17.756
The third strategy is actually using prompt caching and prompt.

00:09:17.756 --> 00:09:24.316
The main idea for prompt caching is that many of the prompt
templates of the prompts that you are using

00:09:24.356 --> 00:09:27.836
have a static and repetitive part, right?

00:09:27.876 --> 00:09:29.836
So this could be, for example,

00:09:30.596 --> 00:09:34.836
many different instructions, a few short examples, even documents.

00:09:34.836 --> 00:09:42.196
Sometimes with prompt caching, what's happening is that you
are actually storing this static part, this fixed part.

00:09:42.196 --> 00:09:45.676
So Bedrock doesn't have to reprocess it over and over again.

00:09:45.676 --> 00:09:51.556
And by doing so, you are able
to have up to 80% faster time to first token.

00:09:51.556 --> 00:09:55.316
And you are you can achieve up to 90% cost savings.

00:09:55.316 --> 00:09:57.876
So this is actually a low hanging fruit.

00:09:59.396 --> 00:10:00.000
The other the fourth strategy is to optimize your prompts.

